mes>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Fuchs</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Intrapartum translabial ultrasound (ITU): sonographic landmark and correlation with successful vacuum extraction</article-title><source>Ultrasound Obstet Gynecol</source><year>2006</year><volume>28</volume><fpage>753</fpage><lpage>760</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17063455</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B13"><label>13</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Eggebo</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name><name><surname>Heien</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Okland</surname><given-names>I</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Ultrasound assessment of fetal head-perineumdistance before induction of labor</article-title><source>Ultrasound Obstet Gynecol</source><year>2008</year><volume>32</volume><fpage>199</fpage><lpage>204</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18528923</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B14"><label>14</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Sakamoto</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Takagi</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Masaoka</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Clinical application of the perineal scan: prepartum screening for cord presentation</article-title><source>Am J Obstet Gynecol</source><year>1986</year><volume>155</volume><fpage>1041</fpage><lpage>1043</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">3535515</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B15"><label>15</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zilianti</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Azuaga</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Calderon</surname><given-names>F</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Transperineal sonography in second trimester to term pregnancy and early labor</article-title><source>J Ultrasound Med</source><year>1991</year><volume>10</volume><fpage>481</fpage><lpage>485</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">1920587</pub-id></element-citation></ref><ref id="B16"><label>16</label><element-citation publication-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Weber</surname><given-names>TM</given-names></name><name><surname>Hertzberg</surname><given-names>BS</given-names></name><name><surname>Bowie</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Transperineal US: alternative technique to improve visualization of the presenting part</article-title><source>Radiology</source><year>1991</year><volume>179</volume><fpage>747</fpage><lpage>750</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">2027986</pub-id></element-citation></ref></ref-list></back></article>